{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjvV5rJ6nFYfJzmzCou","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking a technical question about PostgreSQL advisory locks and what happens when:\n1. A session holding an advisory lock dies mid-transaction\n2. Whether the next waiter gets the lock immediately or has to wait for TCP keepalive\n\nThis is a conceptual/understanding question about PostgreSQL behavior, not asking me to code or change anything. They explicitly say they're not touching code yet - they want to understand the failure mode.\n\nThis is a great question that requires understanding of:\n- PostgreSQL advisory lock semantics\n- Session lifecycle\n- TCP connection handling\n- Lock release behavior\n\nLet me think through this:\n\n1. **Advisory locks are session-scoped**: Advisory locks are held by a PostgreSQL session. When the session ends, all advisory locks held by that session are automatically released.\n\n2. **When a session dies mid-transaction**: If a session holding an advisory lock dies (connection terminates, crash, etc.), PostgreSQL will:\n   - End the session\n   - Rollback any open transaction\n   - Release all locks held by that session, including advisory locks\n\n3. **The key question about TCP keepalive**: This is the important part. The behavior depends on HOW the session dies:\n   - If the connection is cleanly closed (client sends disconnect), PostgreSQL knows immediately\n   - If the connection is abruptly terminated (network failure, process crash), PostgreSQL may not know immediately\n   - TCP keepalive is used to detect dead connections, but this adds delay\n\n4. **What actually happens with advisory locks**: Advisory locks are released as soon as PostgreSQL's backend process detects that the session has ended. This doesn't require the client to acknowledge anything - it's based on PostgreSQL's internal tracking.\n\n5. **TCP keepalive timing**: By default, TCP keepalive probes start after 2 hours of inactivity on Linux, so that's NOT the factor here. However:\n   - If the connection dies abnormally, PostgreSQL uses `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` (default unlimited) to detect sessions\n   - The OS-level TCP keepalive settings (`tcp_keepalives_idle`, `tcp_keepalives_interval`, `tcp_keepalives_count`) can also play a role in detecting dead connections\n\n6. **The real answer**: For an abruptly terminated connection, PostgreSQL won't immediately know the session is gone. The lock release depends on detection mechanisms: either a clean disconnect (immediate), TCP keepalive probes eventually timing out, or `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` triggering if the session is idle in a transaction.\n\n7. **For waiters**: Other sessions waiting on the lock will remain blocked if the lock holder is a zombie process. They're stuck until PostgreSQL detects the dead connection through TCP keepalive, a configured timeout like `statement_timeout`, or manual intervention with `pg_terminate_backend()`. The practical issue is that a crashed session can hold locks indefinitely, blocking other work until one of these detection mechanisms kicks in.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6970,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6970},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"3f354adb-b041-4478-a8a7-dad0c1727e85","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:46:11.139Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjvTboURPEWMaGvLyj2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjvV5rJ6nFYfJzmzCou","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Straight answer: **Advisory locks release immediately when PostgreSQL's backend process detects the session is gone, but there's a dangerous gap if the connection dies abnormally.**\n\nHere's the failure mode:\n\n**Clean disconnect:** Lock released right away. The client sends FIN, PostgreSQL processes it, cleans up the session, advisory locks go free, next waiter wakes up. No delay.\n\n**Abnormal death** (process crash, network partition, pull-the-cable scenario): PostgreSQL doesn't know the connection is dead yet. The backend process is still alive. The lock is still held. **Waiters remain blocked.** This persists until one of:\n\n1. **TCP keepalive detects it** — but this is slow. Linux default is 2 hours before the first probe. Even with aggressive tuning (PostgreSQL's `tcp_keepalives_idle`, `tcp_keepalives_interval`, `tcp_keepalives_count`), you're looking at minutes in a best case, not seconds.\n\n2. **`idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` fires** — this detects an idle transaction and kills the backend. But only if you've set it; default is unlimited. And only if there's an open transaction; an advisory lock outside a txn doesn't help.\n\n3. **Someone manually terminates the backend** — `pg_terminate_backend(pid)`. The lock releases as soon as that runs.\n\n**The real trap:** If the lock holder crashed with an open transaction but no `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` configured, waiters can hang indefinitely. The socket looks \"open\" from PostgreSQL's perspective until TCP keepalive gives up or someone kills it.\n\n**What you likely need to know:** Is there a timeout configured on the lock-acquiring side? If a waiter hits `statement_timeout` or a connection timeout before the lock holder's session gets detected/terminated, that waiter fails — but the lock stays held by the zombie. That's where cascading failures happen."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6970,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6970},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"a39fc495-0480-472e-835e-e63b3e5c1da1","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:46:15.647Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjvTboURPEWMaGvLyj2"}
